<ExtractVariables name="EV-TokenUsage" continueOnError="true" enabled="true">
  <DisplayName>Extract token usage from the model response</DisplayName>
  <!--
    WHY THIS EXISTS, and it is probably a bug fix rather than an addition.

    LTQ-Count previously took its usage straight from a message template:

      <LLMTokenUsageSource>{jsonPath($.usageMetadata.candidatesTokenCount,response.content,true)}</LLMTokenUsageSource>

    That is the SAME mechanism proven NOT to work against the Vertex response
    body in an Apigee debug session on 2026-09-15: message-template jsonPath()
    returns nothing here even when the body is present and the path is correct.
    Three syntaxes were tried live and all resolved empty (see
    apigee/disabled/model-armor/EV-ModelResponseText.xml for the evidence).

    If that is true for LTQ-Count too, the LLM token quota has been counting
    ZERO on every call since it was deployed, and the daily budget has never
    incremented. That would be invisible: the calls succeed, the counter simply
    never moves, and a quota that never fills looks exactly like a quota that is
    never reached. CONFIRM THIS in a debug session; it is the single most
    valuable thing to check when this change is deployed.

    ExtractVariables uses a different JSONPath implementation and demonstrably
    works in this proxy (EV-ModelFromPath). So extraction happens here and the
    consumers read plain flow variables.

    Vertex generateContent responses carry:
      usageMetadata.promptTokenCount      input
      usageMetadata.candidatesTokenCount  output
      usageMetadata.totalTokenCount       both

    All three are extracted: total drives the quota, and the input/output split
    is logged by ML-Attribution because they are billed at different rates, so
    a single total cannot be turned back into a cost.

    continueOnError is true deliberately. A missing usage field must not fail a
    call that the model already answered and that the user is already paying
    for. The cost of that choice is that a broken extraction degrades to
    counting zero rather than erroring, which is precisely the failure this
    policy exists to fix, so it must be verified rather than assumed.
  -->
  <Source>response</Source>
  <JSONPayload>
    <Variable name="prompt_tokens" type="integer">
      <JSONPath>$.usageMetadata.promptTokenCount</JSONPath>
    </Variable>
    <Variable name="candidates_tokens" type="integer">
      <JSONPath>$.usageMetadata.candidatesTokenCount</JSONPath>
    </Variable>
    <Variable name="total_tokens" type="integer">
      <JSONPath>$.usageMetadata.totalTokenCount</JSONPath>
    </Variable>
  </JSONPayload>
  <VariablePrefix>llm</VariablePrefix>
  <IgnoreUnresolvedVariables>true</IgnoreUnresolvedVariables>
</ExtractVariables>
